    15	# ADR-190 — Propriedade de superfície condicional é decidida pela TABELA, executada pelos scripts
    16	
    17	## §1 Contexto
    18	
    19	O PLAN-167 (`7c0828a`) substituiu dezenas de `if`s espalhados por
    20	`install.sh`/`upgrade.sh` por uma decisão única: `_ownership_verdict()` em
    21	`scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`, função **pura** das dimensões
    22	observadas, devolvendo `"<VERDICT> <HASH_SOURCE>"`. Os scripts **observam →
    23	chamam → executam**; eles não decidem. O PLAN-168 fechou os follow-ups
    24	(fiação de CI, INV-4, este ADR). Este registro existe para que a próxima
    25	pessoa que "conserte uma assimetria" tenha onde ler que ela é **decidida**,
    26	não acidental.
    27	
    28	Autoridades, em ordem:
    29	- **Valores:** `scripts/tests/ownership_table.tsv` — "THIS FILE IS THE TRUTH".
    30	- **Racional e legalidade:** `docs/ownership-decision-table.md`.
    31	- **Decisão executável:** `_ownership_verdict()` — e SÓ ela.
    32	- **Oráculos:** `test-ownership-verdict-unit.sh` (decisão, milissegundos) e
    33	  `test-ownership-table.sh` (observação/execução, ~25 min de installs reais);
    34	  `test-protocol-pointer-inv4.sh` (INV-4 executável). CI: unit + controles
    35	  por-PR em `smoke-install.yml`; e2e nightly em `ownership-nightly.yml` com
    36	  gate de conjunto (`ownership-nightly-gate.sh` vs
    37	  `ownership-expected-reds.txt`).
    38	
    39	## §2 Decisão
    40	
    41	### §2.1 As 10 dimensões
    42	
    43	`surface · prior_record · live_type · live_content · source_has · mode ·
    44	ceremony · operation · skip_requested · fault` — definidas, com domínios e
    45	regras de legalidade (R-01..R-10), em `docs/ownership-decision-table.md` §2.
    46	Uma célula é um ponto legal desse produto; a TSV enumera as classes de
    47	equivalência (R-10).
    48	
    49	### §2.2 O enum final tem QUATRO vereditos
    50	
    51	`DELIVER · REFRESH · PRESERVE_OWNED · PRESERVE_UNOWNED`
    52	
    53	- A OQ-9 (ratificada pelo Owner, 2026-08-07) colapsou `OMIT_RECORD` em
    54	  `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`: os dois diziam "sem registro no disco" e diferiam só
    55	  pela coluna `prior_record` — membro redundante de enum é onde dois ramos
    56	  discordam sobre qual se aplica.
    57	- **`ABORT_SURFACE` NÃO é veredito.** É resultado de OBSERVAÇÃO/EXECUÇÃO do
    58	  harness (INV-3: falha de execução nunca avança o registro). A função nunca
    59	  o devolve; um ADR que o listasse como veredito contradiria o código.
    60	
    61	### §2.3 As quatro invariantes cross-surface (INV-1..4)
    62	
    63	- **INV-1** — continuidade em install rerun não muda digest registrado fora
    64	  do conjunto de continuidade.
    65	- **INV-2** — serialização `LINK` só cobre paths que JÁ eram `LINK` antes do
    66	  run (o symlink do adotante nunca vira registro de entrega).
    67	- **INV-3** — falha de execução nunca avança o registro (`ABORT_SURFACE` é
    68	  esse evento, nomeado).
    69	- **INV-4** — install e upgrade geram conteúdo **byte-idêntico** para a mesma
    70	  superfície. Fechada pelo PLAN-168 W2: o ponteiro `PROTOCOL.md` é gerado
    71	  pela ÚNICA função compartilhada (`_render_protocol_pointer*` em
    72	  `_framework_manifest_set.sh`); heredoc privado em caller é REGRESSÃO desta
    73	  invariante. Corolário pós-INV-4: em linhas de continuidade o digest
    74	  canônico e o prior record são os MESMOS bytes — `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` e
    75	  `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` colapsam num só claim, e o harness trata os dois
    76	  nomes como equivalentes SÓ quando os candidatos aliasam
    77	  (docs §2.4, "Hash-name aliasing").
    78	
    79	### §2.4 A assimetria deliberada SPEC vs PROTOCOL
    80	
    81	- **`SPEC/v1` editado = FORK** ⇒ a rota forçada **refresha** (é o contrato de
    82	  compliance publicado; ADR-155-AMEND-1 §4).
    83	- **`PROTOCOL.md` editado = CONTEÚDO do adotante** ⇒ **preserva** (prosa
    84	  editável; sobrescrever é a perda S238 que a decisão (iii) do ADR-155
    85	  fechou).
    86	
    87	É a assimetria que mais convida um "conserto" futuro. Ela é **decidida**.
    88	Quem quiser mudá-la emenda ESTE ADR e refaz o debate — não "alinha" os dois
    89	ramos num PR.
    90	
    91	- **`degraded` (PLAN-168 W2) não é exceção à preservação de `edited`:** é a
    92	  constatação de que o corpo com `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` literal é lixo que o
    93	  PRÓPRIO framework produziu (upgrade pré-fix). Reconhecimento por
    94	  **reconstrução exata de template** (nunca substring, nunca hash estático —
    95	  o corpo embute TARGET/PROFILE/STACK da invocação), qualquer desvio ⇒
    96	  `edited` ⇒ preservado. Cura = `REFRESH` com backup. Doutrina r20
    97	  (`legacy_pristine`) aplicada ao ponteiro; célula própria na TSV
    98	  (OWN-0092..0094), R-04b.
    99	
   100	### §2.5 Relação com o ADR-155-AMEND-1
   101	
   102	**Emendado, não revogado.** A enumeração compartilhada (decisão (i)) e a
   103	propriedade por registro de entrega continuam válidas; este ADR acrescenta o
   104	contrato da DECISÃO (tabela + função + oráculos) e a INV-4 sobre o CONTEÚDO
   105	que os dois lados produzem.
   106	
   107	### §2.6 Células conhecidas-abertas (estado ao aceitar este ADR)
   108	
   109	Abertas — **3**, com causa, protegidas pelo gate de conjunto (encolher =
   110	falha; verde-total = a tabela mudou ⇒ PARAR):
   111	- `OWN-0016` — defeito de PRODUTO (aberto).
   112	- `OWN-0024` / `OWN-0027` — defeitos do TESTE (células de fault de execução
   113	  que o harness ainda não instancia fielmente).
   114	
   115	Fechada — histórico, não expectativa:
   116	- `OWN-0074` — defeito de PRODUTO fechado pelo PLAN-168 W2: era a INV-4 se
   117	  manifestando no digest gravado (`HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` que não batia com
   118	  o disco). Um ADR que a listasse como aberta nasceria stale.
   119	
   120	## §3 Consequências
   121	
   122	- Adicionar um ramo que decide propriedade LOCALMENTE em
   123	  `install.sh`/`upgrade.sh`/`doctor.sh`/`uninstall.sh` reabre a classe que o
   124	  PLAN-167 fechou — é veto de revisão, não estilo.
   125	- Mudanças na tabela/enum/dimensões exigem: debate L3 + atualização em
   126	  `docs/ownership-decision-table.md` + TSV + oráculos verdes + este ADR
   127	  emendado. Os quatro andam juntos ou a mudança não anda.
   128	- O conjunto esperado de vermelhos (`ownership-expected-reds.txt`) é parte do
   129	  contrato: o CI compara o CONJUNTO exato (nunca `--map`, nunca rc cru) e
   130	  qualquer status TIMEOUT/ESCAPE/AMBIG falha imediatamente.
   131	
   132	## §4 Blast radius
   133	
   134	`scripts/install.sh` · `scripts/upgrade.sh` · `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`
   135	· `scripts/doctor.sh` · `scripts/uninstall.sh` · os 4 oráculos/gates de teste ·
   136	`smoke-install.yml` · `ownership-nightly.yml` · adotantes em campo (a cura do
   137	degraded reescreve, com backup, um arquivo que upgrades antigos corromperam).
     1	# Ownership decision table — install/upgrade conditional surfaces
     2	
     3	> **Status (reconciled 2026-08-09, PLAN-169 — repass-r2 part-e P1): the
     4	> debate HAPPENED and this document's draft framing is HISTORICAL.** The
     5	> ratified state lives in **ADR-190** (the contract summary) and
     6	> `scripts/tests/ownership_table.tsv` (the truth): the verdict enum
     7	> collapsed from six to **FOUR** (OQ-9 folded `OMIT_RECORD` into
     8	> `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`; Owner ratification in PLAN-167 §9), the two
     9	> "committed on purpose" inconsistencies below were RESOLVED by that
    10	> ratification, and **INV-4 (§5.4e) was CLOSED by PLAN-168 W2** — the
    11	> pointer is generated by the ONE shared generator; §5.4e stays as the
    12	> discovery record, not an open defect. The body below is preserved as
    13	> the reasoning trail that produced the table; where it disagrees with
    14	> ADR-190, ADR-190 wins.
    15	>
    16	> *Original W0 framing (historical):* the enum in §3 was a draft until
    17	> the PLAN-167 W1 debate; the open questions in §6 were the debate's
    18	> agenda.
    19	>
    20	> **Two known inconsistencies are committed on purpose.** Writing the decision
    21	> function against this table (the W2 proposal) exposed them, and resolving
    22	> them unilaterally would pre-decide exactly what the debate exists to settle:
    23	>
    24	> 1. **The table is not self-consistent about `OMIT_RECORD`.** Some rows with a
    25	>    prior record expect `OMIT_RECORD`, others with a prior record expect
    26	>    `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`. Sixty-one hand-written rows never had to agree with
    27	>    each other; a function has to. See OQ-9.
    28	> 2. **`OWN-0018` and `OWN-0020` have identical dimensions and opposite
    29	>    outcomes**, separated only by a prose directive in `note`. A decision
    30	>    function cannot read prose, so the distinction has to become a *value* —
    31	>    the proposal is a `live_content` of `legacy_pristine_partial`, meaning a
    32	>    tree carrying an entry the fingerprint cannot hash. See OQ-7.
    33	>
    34	> The baseline map was measured against the table in this state, so the map and
    35	> the table agree with each other. Reconciling either one is W2 work, after
    36	> ratification.
    37	
    38	## 1. What this document is, and why it exists
    39	
    40	`ADR-155-AMEND-1` decided that framework ownership of the three
    41	**conditional** surfaces — root `PROTOCOL.md`, the `SPEC/v1` contract tree,
    42	and the `.claude/.framework-version` marker — derives from the **registered
    43	delivery record**, never from the ceremony alone and never from file
    44	presence.
    45	
    46	The decision was right. Its *realization* was a cascade of `if` branches
    47	spread across `scripts/install.sh`, `scripts/upgrade.sh` and
    48	`scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`. That shape has a measured failure
    49	mode: over eleven consecutive cross-model review rounds on the same code,
    50	**35 distinct defects** were reported, and roughly half of the later ones
    51	were regressions introduced by the fix for the round immediately before.
    52	The 45-check end-to-end suite stayed green throughout — it exercises eight
    53	scenarios, and the decision space is a nine-dimensional product.
    54	
    55	The cause is not carelessness. It is that **"correct" was being decided one
    56	branch at a time**, so two branches could encode contradictory answers to
    57	the same question and nothing would notice. This document makes the space
    58	explicit so that contradictions surface *before* they become defects.
    59	
    60	Division of labour, strictly observed:
    61	
    62	| Artifact | Role |
    63	|---|---|
    64	| this document | defines the dimensions, the legality rules, and the reasoning |
    65	| `scripts/tests/ownership_table.tsv` | **the truth** — one row per legal cell, with its expected verdict |
    66	| `scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh` | executes every row against the **real** scripts |
    67	
    68	**No value is duplicated between this document and the TSV.** If you want to
    69	know what a given cell decides, read the TSV. If you want to know *why the
    70	cell exists at all*, read this.
    71	
    72	## 2. The nine dimensions
    73	
    74	A "cell" is one assignment of all nine. The subject under test is one
    75	surface's outcome on one run.
    76	
    77	### 2.1 `surface`
    78	
    79	| Value | Path | Shape |
    80	|---|---|---|
    81	| `spec` | `SPEC/v1` | a **tree** |
    82	| `protocol` | root `PROTOCOL.md` | a **generated pointer** — no source file; the body is a heredoc built from `$SOURCE_DIR`/`$TARGET`/`$PROFILE`/`$STACK` |
    83	| `marker` | `.claude/.framework-version` | a tracked single-line file |
    84	
    85	The three differ in more than their path, and every difference has produced
    86	at least one defect. `spec` is the only tree (so it is the only surface with
    87	descendants, per-file records, and a "partially populated" state);
    88	`protocol` is the only surface with no bytes in the source (so `source_has`
    89	is meaningless for it, and its baseline digest comes from a *computed*
    90	canonical hash); `marker` is the only surface inside `.claude/`, so it is
    91	the only one both ceremonies receive.
    92	
    93	### 2.2 `prior_record` — what the PRE-run baseline manifest says
    94	
    95	| Value | Meaning |
    96	|---|---|
    97	| `none` | no record line for this relpath |
    98	| `hash` | a `<64-hex>  <relpath>` record |
    99	| `link_match` | a `LINK  <relpath>  <target>` record whose target equals the live `readlink` |
   100	| `link_retargeted` | a `LINK` record whose target does **not** equal the live `readlink` |
   101	
   102	This dimension describes the **previous** run's testimony, read before
   103	anything is written this run. It is the operative meaning of "registered
   104	delivery" in ADR-155-AMEND-1 §3.
   105	
   106	### 2.3 `live_type` — `lstat` of the destination, never following
   107	
   108	`absent` · `dir` · `dir_empty` · `regular` · `symlink` · `special`
   109	(FIFO, socket, device) · `ancestor_symlink`.
   110	
   111	`ancestor_symlink` is a value of this dimension rather than a tenth
   112	dimension because the decision function short-circuits on it *before* it
   113	ever looks at the leaf — exactly as it does for `special`. It answers the
   114	same question ("what kind of destination is this?") with "one reachable only
   115	by writing through a symlink out of the target tree".
   116	
   117	`dir_empty` is split from `dir` because for `spec` it changes the *manifest*
   118	outcome without changing the *filesystem* outcome — the enumeration walks
   119	the target and emits a record per regular file, so an empty tree yields no
   120	records at all even when every other signal says "owned".
   121	
   122	`special` exists because two separate defects were `cp` invoked on a FIFO,
   123	which blocks forever waiting for a writer and hangs the run **mid-upgrade**,
   124	after earlier surfaces have already been modified.
   125	
   126	### 2.4 `live_content` — only defined when `live_type ∈ {dir, dir_empty, regular}`
   127	
   128	| Value | Meaning |
   129	|---|---|
   130	| `pristine` | byte-identical to what **this** source would deliver |
   131	| `legacy_pristine` | matches a `SPEC/v1` fingerprint the framework shipped at v1.2.0 or earlier |
   132	| `degraded` | (protocol only, PLAN-168 W2) byte-exact reconstruction of the `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}`-literal pointer template a pre-PLAN-168 `upgrade.sh` wrote — the framework's OWN output, never adopter content |
   133	| `edited` | none of the above |
   134	
   135	`legacy_pristine` exists because v1.2-and-earlier installs never enumerated
   136	`SPEC/v1`, so no record can distinguish a framework-installed tree from an
   137	adopter-authored one; the ambiguity is resolved by content against three
   138	pinned fingerprints.
   139	
   140	`degraded` is the same doctrine applied to the pointer (the r20 precedent):
   141	the broken upgrade left `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` literal while embedding the
   142	invocation's `TARGET`/`PROFILE`/`STACK`, so recognition is by **template
   143	reconstruction** — extract the invocation values from the file's own upgrade
   144	line, re-render the one shipped template (identical across v1.0.1→HEAD,
   145	verified by extraction), and require byte equality. Substring matching is
   146	forbidden (an adopter file that legitimately *contains* the token would be
   147	destroyed); a static whole-body hash is useless (each adopter's degraded
   148	body embeds different values). Any parse failure or deviation ⇒ `edited` ⇒
   149	preserved. Recognizer: `_protocol_pointer_is_degraded` in
   150	`scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`.
   151	
   152	**Hash-name aliasing (post-INV-4).** Once install and upgrade render through
   153	the one generator, the canonical pointer digest and the prior record are the
   154	SAME bytes on continuity rows — `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` and
   155	`HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` collapse into one claim. The e2e harness therefore
   156	treats the two names as equivalent **only when the candidate digests are
   157	equal and the record matches them** (`_derive_hash_source`, 5th argument);
   158	when the digests genuinely differ, the probe order decides exactly as
   159	before. Choosing by probe order in the aliased case would manufacture a
   160	distinction the observation cannot make.
   161	
   162	### 2.5 `source_has` — does `$SOURCE_DIR` carry this surface?
   163	
   164	`yes` · `no`. The reachable `no` case is the documented `--pin` downgrade to
   165	a pre-v1.3.0 tag, whose checkout has no marker. A `SPEC/v1` absent from
   166	source means a broken or partial checkout.
   167	
   168	### 2.6 `mode` — the delivery mode of **this** run
   169	
   170	`copy` · `link`. On `install.sh` this is `--mode`. On `upgrade.sh` there is
   171	no `--mode` flag: the value is *inferred* for the baseline rewrite, from a
   172	prior `LINK` record first and a symlink probe second.
   173	
   174	> **This dimension is about the current run, not the recorded one.** See
   175	> pruning rule R-09 for why conflating them would delete a real defect.
   176	
   177	### 2.7 `ceremony`
   178	
   179	`user` · `maintainer` — the *effective* ceremony, read replay-independently
   180	from `.claude/.install-state.json`, failing open to `maintainer` when the
   380	destroyed a file the test never looked at.
   381	
   382	The fixture's foreign file is now a **tripwire**, digested before and after
   383	every run. Any change to it produces status `ESCAPE`, which outranks the
   384	verdict comparison entirely — a row whose pair matches while the run wrote
   385	out of tree has not passed.
   386	
   387	Arming it immediately converted a suspicion into evidence. `OWN-0034` — the
   388	`protocol` surface as a leaf symlink — reports `ESCAPE`: `cat >` follows
   389	the link and writes outside the target. `OWN-0044` (a `spec` symlink, which
   390	is correctly preserved) does not, so the tripwire is not simply firing on
   391	every symlink row.
   392	
   393	That promotes the §5.1 finding. The missing leaf-symlink guard on the
   394	pointer is not a hypothetical hardening gap; **it is a demonstrated
   395	out-of-tree write**, which is the S238 class the whole baseline-manifest
   396	design exists to close.
   397	
   398	### 5.4c `prior_record` is ambiguous, and it matters exactly where it hurts
   399	
   400	Running the decision function in shadow mode against the real callers — it
   401	observes and records, it does not act — produced 17 agreements, 2
   402	divergences and 10 rows the caller never reached. One divergence is a model
   403	defect, not an implementation defect.
   404	
   405	`prior_record` is defined as "what the pre-run baseline manifest says". There
   406	are **two** such manifests and the definition does not choose between them:
   407	
   408	- the **raw** file on disk, and
   409	- the **sanitized** one the loader produces, which drops every record whose
   410	  relpath traverses a symlinked component.
   411	
   412	They agree everywhere except on the symlink-traversal rows — which are the
   413	security-critical ones, and the same rows §5.8 is about. An observer reading
   414	the sanitized manifest sees `none` and concludes `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`; an
   415	observer reading the raw file sees `hash` and concludes `OMIT_RECORD`. Both
   416	are defensible readings of a dimension that never said which it meant.
   417	
   418	The resolution is not to pick the more convenient one. The **sanitized**
   419	manifest is the authority, because honouring a record whose path crosses a
   420	symlink is precisely what the ADR-155 decision-(v) provenance fence exists
   421	to prevent — but the definition in §2.2 has to say so, and the harness has
   422	to observe the same thing the caller does, or the two instruments will keep
   423	disagreeing about cells neither of them is wrong about.
   424	
   425	This is the kind of defect that survives eleven rounds of code review: every
   426	branch reads *a* manifest, each reads a defensible one, and no branch is
   427	individually wrong.
   428	
   429	### 5.4d The missing cell was the most important one
   430	
   431	The table had nine `protocol` rows and none for the combination that matters
   432	most: an **adopter-customised pointer on a normal (maintainer) upgrade**.
   433	`OWN-0072` covers the same content under `ceremony=user`; the ordinary path
   434	was simply absent.
   435	
   436	Deriving its expected pair exposed a **data-loss defect in the proposed
   437	decision function**. For that cell the function returned `REFRESH` — it would
   438	have overwritten a customised root `PROTOCOL.md`. That is the verified S238
   439	loss that ADR-155 decision (iii) exists to close, and the live code has
   440	preserved it correctly all along.
   441	
   442	The asymmetry the function was missing is deliberate and is now stated in it:
   443	
   444	| Surface | An adopter edit is… | Because |
   445	|---|---|---|
   446	| `SPEC/v1` | a **fork of the contract** → forced refresh | it is the published compliance contract (ADR-155-AMEND-1 §4) |
   447	| `PROTOCOL.md` | **adopter content** → preserved | overwriting it is the verified S238 loss (ADR-155 (iii)) |
   448	
   449	Both record a **canonical** digest regardless, because recording the
   450	customised bytes would make the *next* upgrade read `H_dst == H_base` and
   451	clobber them — the C.5 idempotency trap.
   452	
   453	Two things are worth separating here. The defect was in the **new** code, not
   454	the old: a refactor that had been driven only by "keep the map green" would
   455	have shipped it, because **no existing row covered the cell**. What found it
   456	was asking the completeness question — *which combinations does this surface
   457	have, and is each one present?* — which is the one question a per-branch
   458	review never asks.
   459	
   460	### 5.4e Install and upgrade generate DIFFERENT pointers — every upgrade breaks it (CLOSED by PLAN-168 W2 — INV-4; discovery record below)
   461	
   462	Chasing a `HASH_UNCLASSIFIED` observation led to a defect in the live code
   463	that has nothing to do with ownership records.
   464	
   465	`install.sh` writes the root pointer and then **substitutes** its
   466	placeholders, so the adopter gets a real path. `upgrade.sh` regenerates the
   467	same file from a heredoc that leaves `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` **literal**, and
   468	nothing substitutes it afterwards. Verified against the live tree:
   469	
   470	| after | literal `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` occurrences |
   471	|---|---:|
   472	| `install.sh` | 0 — the file names the real checkout path |
   473	| `upgrade.sh` | 4 — the file names a placeholder |
   474	
   475	So **every upgrade degrades the pointer into a non-functional one** for any
   476	adopter whose framework checkout lives outside the target. The file whose
   477	entire job is to say where the protocol lives stops saying it.
   478	
   479	This is the **install-set ≠ upgrade-set** class that ADR-155 decision (i) was
   480	written to eliminate — the shared enumeration fixed *which paths* the two
   481	sides touch, but not *what content* they produce for the same path. It is
   482	pre-existing, unrelated to PLAN-167's changes, and no ownership assertion
   483	would ever have caught it: the record is fine, the bytes are wrong.
   484	
   485	It is recorded as **INV-4** because it is not a cell — it is a cross-writer
   486	invariant.
   487	
   488	### 5.5 Two findings are invariants, not cells
   489	
   490	Two defects were about the **blast radius** of a fix rather than about any
   491	one surface, and encoding them as rows would understate them. The harness
   492	asserts them across every applicable row instead:
   493	
   494	- **INV-1** — when ownership continuity fires on an install rerun, no
   495	  enumerated path *outside* the continuity set may change its recorded
   496	  digest. The original defect switched the whole tree's baseline to the
   497	  unrendered source, which reads downstream as repo-wide adopter drift and
   498	  silently stops those files from ever being refreshed again. One row could
   499	  not have caught it; the damage was to the paths the row was not about.
   500	- **INV-2** — `LINK` serialization may cover only paths that were already
   501	  `LINK` records before the run. Otherwise an adopter's own symlink,
   502	  preserved inside an enumerated directory, is promoted into a framework
   503	  delivery record.
   504	- **INV-4** — **install and upgrade must generate byte-identical content for
   505	  the same surface.** Violated today by the root pointer (§5.4e): install
   506	  substitutes its placeholders, upgrade does not. No per-cell ownership
   507	  assertion can see this — the record is correct and the bytes are wrong —
   508	  so it needs an invariant that compares the two writers' output directly.
   509	- **INV-3** — **an execution failure never advances the record.** A caller
   510	  that was handed `DELIVER` or `REFRESH` and could not complete it must
   511	  leave the manifest describing the world as it actually is. This is the
   512	  correction to the C2 proposal below: "the failure inherits the verdict's
   513	  `hash_source`" would record a delivery that did not happen — the framework
   514	  claiming bytes it never wrote, which is precisely the over-claiming
   515	  direction ADR-155-AMEND-1 §3 forbids. The failure path keeps the record
   516	  the surface had *before* the attempt.
   517	
   518	Both are the same shape: a switch that was correct in intent and too wide in
   519	scope. That is the single most common defect class in this space, and it is
   520	invisible to per-cell assertions by construction.

